    "abstract": "The ensemble average projections of the Coupled Model Inter-comparison Project Phase 5 (CMIP5) ensemble show future increases in shortwave radiation at the surface (SW) in Japan. We reveal that the Arctic Oscillation-like atmospheric circulation trends cause cloud cover decreases around Japan, leading to increases in the SW.",
    "field": "climate change projections",
    "problem": "The common practice of using small, biased subsets of climate models for impact assessments, such as Japan's S-8 project, fails to capture the full uncertainty range for future surface solar radiation projections, leading to potentially skewed results.",
    "method": "To select a better Global Climate Model (GCM) subset, we propose a method that maximizes the mean distance between models in the rank space of seasonal surface solar radiation projections, ensuring the selected models are widely distributed and not biased within the full ensemble uncertainty range.",
    "task": "The task is to develop and apply a method for selecting an unbiased and representative subset of climate models to project future surface solar radiation changes in Japan, addressing biases found in previously used model selections.",
